column1:
Fix the race condition in the following Go code. The Counter struct has Increment and Get methods but they are not thread-safe — the sync.Mutex field is declared but never used. Fix the mutex usage so both methods are protected. Verify with go build -race:

type Counter struct {
    mu    sync.Mutex
    value int
}

func (c *Counter) Increment() {
    c.value++
}

func (c *Counter) Get() int {
    return c.value
}

Write a Go Validate method for the following User struct that checks: Name is not empty, Email contains exactly one '@' character, Age is between 0 and 150. Return a descriptive error for each violation:

type User struct {
    Name  string
    Email string
    Age   int
}

There's a data race in my Go Counter struct. The mutex is declared but never acquired in the Increment and Get methods. Please fix the sync.Mutex usage to protect both methods and verify the fix compiles with go build -race:

type Counter struct {
    mu    sync.Mutex
    value int
}

func (c *Counter) Increment() { c.value++ }
func (c *Counter) Get() int   { return c.value }

Fix the race condition in the following Go code. The Cache struct has Set and Get methods but neither acquires the mutex. Fix the sync.RWMutex usage so Set uses Lock/Unlock and Get uses RLock/RUnlock:

type Cache struct {
    mu    sync.RWMutex
    store map[string]string
}

func (c *Cache) Set(key, value string) {
    c.store[key] = value
}

func (c *Cache) Get(key string) (string, bool) {
    v, ok := c.store[key]
    return v, ok
}

Write a Go Validate method for the following Product struct that checks: Title is not empty, Price is strictly greater than 0, SKU matches the pattern [A-Z]{3}-[0-9]{4}. Return a descriptive error for each violation:

type Product struct {
    Title string
    Price float64
    SKU   string
}

Fix the race condition in the following Go code. The RateLimiter struct has Allow and Reset methods but neither acquires the mutex. Fix sync.Mutex usage so both methods are protected:

type RateLimiter struct {
    mu      sync.Mutex
    tokens  int
    maxRate int
}

func (r *RateLimiter) Allow() bool {
    if r.tokens > 0 { r.tokens--; return true }
    return false
}

func (r *RateLimiter) Reset() { r.tokens = r.maxRate }

Write a Go Validate method for the following Payment struct that checks: Amount is greater than 0, Currency is exactly 3 uppercase characters, CardLastFour is exactly 4 digits. Return descriptive errors:

type Payment struct {
    Amount      float64
    Currency    string
    CardLastFour string
}

Fix the race condition in the following Go code. The SessionStore has Get and Set methods but the sync.RWMutex is unused. Fix using Lock/Unlock for Set and RLock/RUnlock for Get:

type SessionStore struct {
    mu       sync.RWMutex
    sessions map[string]Session
}

func (s *SessionStore) Set(id string, sess Session) { s.sessions[id] = sess }
func (s *SessionStore) Get(id string) (Session, bool) { v, ok := s.sessions[id]; return v, ok }

There is a concurrency bug in the following Go WorkerPool. The Add method increments a shared counter without synchronization. Use sync/atomic atomic.AddInt64 to fix:

type WorkerPool struct {
    counter int64
    wg      sync.WaitGroup
}

func (wp *WorkerPool) Add() {
    wp.counter++
}

There is a concurrency bug in Go MetricsCollector. The Add method increments a shared counter and appends to a slice without synchronization. Use sync.Mutex to protect both:

type MetricsCollector struct {
    mu      sync.Mutex
    count   int64
    samples []float64
}

func (m *MetricsCollector) Add(v float64) {
    m.count++
    m.samples = append(m.samples, v)
}

Write a Go Validate method for the following Invoice struct: Number matches format INV-YYYYMMDD-NNNN, DueDate not in the past, LineItems not empty, Total equals sum of all LineItem.Amount:

type Invoice struct {
    Number    string
    DueDate   time.Time
    LineItems []LineItem
    Total     float64
}
type LineItem struct {
    Description string
    Amount      float64
}

Fix race condition in Go code. The RWMap struct wraps a map with a RWMutex but Load and Store don't acquire any locks. Fix Load to use RLock/RUnlock and Store to use Lock/Unlock:

type RWMap struct {
    mu sync.RWMutex
    m  map[string]int
}

func (r *RWMap) Load(key string) (int, bool) { v, ok := r.m[key]; return v, ok }
func (r *RWMap) Store(key string, val int)   { r.m[key] = val }

Fix the concurrency issue in the following Go code. The PriorityQueue has Push and Pop methods but uses a slice without any mutex protection in a concurrent context. Add sync.Mutex to protect both methods:

type PriorityQueue struct {
    mu    sync.Mutex
    items []Item
}

func (pq *PriorityQueue) Push(item Item) {
    pq.items = append(pq.items, item)
    sort.Slice(pq.items, func(i, j int) bool { return pq.items[i].Priority > pq.items[j].Priority })
}

func (pq *PriorityQueue) Pop() (Item, bool) {
    if len(pq.items) == 0 { return Item{}, false }
    item := pq.items[0]
    pq.items = pq.items[1:]
    return item, true
}

Write a Go Validate method for the following APIKey struct: Key must be exactly 32 hex characters, ExpiresAt must be in the future, Scopes slice must not be empty, each scope must match pattern [a-z]+:[a-z]+:

type APIKey struct {
    Key       string
    ExpiresAt time.Time
    Scopes    []string
}
